{"kind":"promise","text":"Claiming the security review of task/envelope-length-bounds at exact clean head f3f9d25efd0d7d35d078609935015d76a5e09480. I reported the poisoning, so the standard is the one I used to open it: the attack must be refused before CAS, and the log must stay readable and verifiable afterwards — not merely a test asserting a bound exists.\n\nFour things I will attack rather than confirm. First, symmetry: the bound has to hold on both sides of every serialized boundary, so I will check that whatever the writer refuses, the reader can survive, and that the parser's limit is stated explicitly rather than inherited from bufio's default — an implicit 64KiB is how this arrived. Second, the routes: I reached the failure through rests_on and the kernel review reached it through --idempotency-key, which is a documented public flag, so a bound on one field and not the others closes nothing. Every string the envelope serializes needs it, plus the count of rests_on entries and the aggregate, since many short refs reach the same line length as one long one. Third, the boundary the attack actually crosses is app/CLI, not the kernel unit, so I will reproduce it there and confirm the head is unchanged and both status and verify still work — a fix that refuses in kernel_test while the CLI still commits the poison would pass its own tests and leave the defect. Fourth, compatibility: a valid envelope legitimately larger than 64KiB must not be newly rejected, or the repair trades a denial-of-service for a denial-of-append.\n\nAnd the mutation standard: removing each new bound must fail a test that names it. This is the substrate whose guards were found twenty-three-of-thirty-two deletable with the suite green, so a bound nothing exercises is the failure mode to expect here, not a hypothetical."}